<div class="<%= layout_framework_classes %>">
  <div class="<%= layout_framework_title_classes %>">
    <%= render 'framework/page_header', page_name: :theme_authoring %>
  </div>

  <div class="<%= layout_framework_content_classes %>">
    <%= render_framework_intro_paragraph(:theme_authoring) %>

    <div class="space-y-16">
      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'The contract') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            A theme sets token <em>references</em> through the theme-slot mixins and never touches the paint
            pipeline directly. The screen's device mode still decides how every token renders: dither patterns
            on 1-bit, palette images on limited color, solids on full color.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
            <li>Allowed: semantic channel refs (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">theme-slots.semantic-bg/text/stroke/border</code>)</li>
            <li>Allowed: component slot refs (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">theme-slots.bg-slot/text-slot/border-level-slot/border-token-slot</code>)</li>
            <li>Allowed: utility token remaps (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">theme-slots.utility-*</code> and the bulk <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-remap-*</code> helpers)</li>
            <li>Optional: icon paint for adaptive images (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">theme-slots.semantic-icon</code>). When omitted, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">image--adaptive</code> icons follow the theme's semantic text-primary paint.</li>
            <li>Disallowed: root palette overrides (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--white</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--black</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--gray-*</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--color-*</code>)</li>
            <li>Disallowed: framework-owned paint variables (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--bg-*</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--text-*</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--border-*</code>)</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The full vocabulary of channels, slots, and remaps lives on <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :theme_slots) %>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Start from the boilerplate') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">framework/themes/_theme-boilerplate.scss</code> is the
            starting point. A theme is one file: load the theme-slot mixins, emit the framework layer order, open the
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">tn--themes</code> layer, and scope every rule to
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.trmnl .screen--theme-&lt;name&gt;</code>.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Keep <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">@include layers.order;</code> above the layer
            block. Cascade layer order is fixed by whichever stylesheet the browser parses first. Without it, a theme
            link placed before <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">plugins.css</code> loses to the
            framework defaults.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: '@use "../config/layers" as layers;
@use "../mixins/theme-slots" as theme-slots;

@include layers.order;

@layer tn--themes {
    .trmnl .screen--theme-example {
        // 1. Semantic channels: the whole screen in a few lines.
        @include theme-slots.semantic-bg("canvas", "yellow");
        @include theme-slots.semantic-text("text-primary", "black");

        // 2. Component slots: tune specific surfaces.
        @include theme-slots.bg-slot("title-bar", "yellow-40");

        // 3. Utility remaps: re-point the raw bg--/text-- utilities.
        @include theme-slots.utility-remap-grayscale("yellow");

        // Optional: give image--adaptive icons their own paint
        // (they follow text-primary when this is omitted)
        // @include theme-slots.semantic-icon("yellow-20");
    }
}',
            language: 'scss'
          ) %>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4">
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Work in that order: semantic channels first, then component slots, then utility remaps. The shipped
            themes follow the same section order, so they read as reference implementations:
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">black-and-yellow-theme.scss</code> is a dark-side
            grayscale remap, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">white-and-red-theme.scss</code> a
            bright-side remap with one targeted utility exception.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Register and lint') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The file name, the registry id, and the screen class stay in sync:
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">themes/&lt;id&gt;-theme.scss</code> registers as
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">&lt;id&gt;</code> in
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">lib/framework/themes.rb</code> and applies as
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--theme-&lt;id&gt;</code>.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">rake framework:themes:lint</code> enforces the
            contract on every theme file:
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
            <li>The theme files on disk match the registry ids.</li>
            <li>No framework-owned paint variables (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--bg-*</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--text-*</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--border-*</code>).</li>
            <li>No root palette overrides (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--white</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--black</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--gray-*</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--color-*</code>).</li>
            <li>No calls to the deprecated <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">role-token</code> helper.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Compile and ship') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Each theme compiles to its own stylesheet, never into
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">plugins.css</code>. It ships as a second
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">&lt;link&gt;</code> next to the framework, and the
            screen opts in with its theme class; see <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :themes) %> for usage and
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :sass_build) %> for the compile command.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Dark mode') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Themed screens are exempt from the framework's dark-mode remaps: every dark rule carries a
            zero-specificity <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">:where(:not([class*="screen--theme-"]))</code>
            gate, so a theme is a complete color statement and
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--dark-mode</code> has no effect on it.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            A theme that wants a dark variant styles the combination itself, with the same plain mixins as the rest
            of the theme. There is no dark remap to work around, so the device mode still resolves every token:
            dither patterns on 1-bit, palette tiles on limited color, solids on full color.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: '.trmnl .screen--theme-example.screen--dark-mode {
    @include theme-slots.semantic-bg("canvas", "black");
    @include theme-slots.semantic-bg("surface", "black");
    @include theme-slots.semantic-text("text-primary", "yellow");
}',
            language: 'scss'
          ) %>
        </div>

        <div class="<%= message_warning_classes %>">
          <div class="flex items-center w-full">
            <div class="flex-shrink-0 items-center">
              <%= render 'shared/icons/warning' %>
            </div>
            <div class="w-full ml-3">
              <p class="text-sm font-medium">
                <strong>Do not use <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">$raw: true</code> in a theme.</strong>
                It skips the mode pipeline, so the surface renders as one flat color with no dither on 1-bit and no
                palette tile on <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--color-4bwry</code>. The
                framework uses it only for its own unthemed dark defaults.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Expose values to JavaScript') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Themes never ship JavaScript. A theme that wants to hand extra values to plugin code publishes its own
            public <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--*</code> custom properties on the screen,
            and JS reads them back with <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.cssVar()</code>.
            See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_api) %>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <%= render 'framework/page_navigation' %>
  </div>
</div>
